<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bagratuni dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bagratids of Armenia, Bagratids (Armenia), Armenian Bagratids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bagratuni dynasty was an Armenian noble house that rose from among the great princely families of the Arsacid kingdom to become the leading power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Ashot I was recognised as king in 884–885 by both the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and the Byzantine emperor, and the dynasty ruled the restored Kingdom of Armenia, from Ani in its later years, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> annexed it in 1045.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty.jpg" alt="Bagratuni dynasty" width="320" height="537" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagratunis_symbol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from the Parthian form of the Iranian name Bagadata, God-bearer. The family's origin was long debated. The History of Movses Khorenatsi derives the Bagratuni from a Jew named Shambat; Leo and Grigor Khalatyants thought this an attempt to give the dynasty an older origin, and Nikoghayos Mar linked the claim to a Christian preference for descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a>. Josef Markwart, Nikoghayos Adontz and Leo saw their ancestor in Bagadat, a general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a>. Kirill Tumanov proposed a relationship with the Yervanduni, and Grigor Kapantsyan and Rafael Matevosyan sought roots in the Urartian and Manai periods. Modern Armenian historiography is largely unanimous that the origin was Armenian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to Tumanov, the Bagratids first appear as members of the hereditary nobility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in the early 4th century. The Arshakuni dynasty, which ruled Armenia from 52 to 428, granted the family hereditary rights, among them the titles aspet, head of cavalry, which appears to have been ceremonial, and taagakir, the privilege of crowning the Arshakuni kings. Their holdings included the Speri region in the valley of the Chorokhi, known for gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and Tayk. After the Arab invasions of the 7th century, members of the house often bore the title of prince of Armenia but were subordinate to the Muslim ruler appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>.</p><p>The chronicler Stepannos Orbelian reports that Armenian princes, among them the rulers of Syunik, asked the Arab governor to make Ashot Bagratuni king. The kingdom lasted from about 885 until 1045. Bagaran was the capital under Ashot I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> from 928 to 961, and later Ani. From the middle of the 10th century large feudal houses grew stronger and more independent, and new kingdoms arose, in addition to Vaspurakan, in Syunik; Soviet Armenian historiography calls this a period of fragmentation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1042 Constantine Monomakh took the Byzantine throne and, as heir to the will of Hovhannes-Smbat, demanded his inheritance from King Gagik II. Gagik tried diplomacy and then resisted; two Byzantine campaigns in 1044 failed. Monomakh then invited Gagik to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where after a month he agreed to hand Ani over in exchange for Kalon Pelat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> in Cappadocia and a palace in Constantinople. At home, Vahram Sparapet, who held out for resistance, was isolated, and at the beginning of 1045 Ani, and with it the kingdom, surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. Many historians, among them Toumanoff, Adontz and Suny, regard the Bagratuni as the progenitors of the Georgian royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bagratuni dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1080 Ruben, a follower of the last <b>Bagratid</b> king, created a lordship that became the base of the Rubenid dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/armenian-principality-of-cilicia">Armenian Principality of Cilicia</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom was a federation of noble houses and rival kingdoms under the <b>Bagratid</b> king of kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/bagratid-armenia">Bagratid Armenia</a></li><li><blockquote>Under George II and Constantine III the kingdom expanded into Kartli and fought the <b>Armenian Bagratids</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-abkhazia">Kingdom of Abkhazia</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings ruled from Artaxata and later Vagharshapat over powerful hereditary noble houses such as the Mamikonians and <b>Bagratunis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li><li><blockquote>Kars was a strong fortress town on the route from Anatolia to the Caucasus, and its Cathedral of the Holy Apostles, built by King Abas in the 930s-40s when Kars was still the <b>Bagratid</b> capital, dominated the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kars">Kingdom of Kars</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings alternated between rivalry and alliance with the <b>Bagratids</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vaspurakan">Kingdom of Vaspurakan</a></li></ul></section>
